{{Quote|Your species is smarter than you appear.|Lok Durd|<ref name="DoP" />}}
'''Lurmen''' were a diminutive, pacifistic [[species]] from the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[planet]] [[Mygeeto]]. At least one [[Lurmen village|settlement]] of Lurmen was present on the planet [[Maridun]] at the time of the [[Clone Wars]].

==Biology and appearance==
[[File:Lurmen-SW_Propaganda.png|thumb|left|170px|A Lurmen]]
{{Quote|Now who taught you how to leap like a Lurmen, huh?|[[Peli Motto]], to [[Din Grogu|Grogu]]|<ref name="The Mandalorian Chapter 18">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 18: The Mines of Mandalore}}</ref>}}
Lurmen were a [[Sentience|sentient]], [[Color|yellow]]-[[eye]]d [[species]] that stood about one [[meter]] tall on average. Lurmen had two [[Limb|arms]] and legs with five digits on each hand and foot.<ref name="JC" /> Each Lurmen also had a tail, which was held high for balance.<ref name="Join the Battle">''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars: Character Encyclopedia - Join the Battle!]]''</ref> Their bodies were entirely covered in brown or gray [[Hair|fur]], over which they wore [[Clothing|clothes]]. Some members of the species, such as [[Wag Too]], had the ability to roll into a ball to travel at greater speeds.<ref name="JC" /> The [[Lurmen village|Lurmen colonists]] on [[Maridun]] consumed raw [[jogan fruit]] for nutrition.<ref name="DoP" /> Their natural habitat was plains and [[grass]]lands.<ref name="VE" /> At least one Lurmen was [[Force-sensitive]].<ref name="ITD">''[[The High Republic: Into the Dark]]''</ref>

==Society and culture==
{{Quote|We will not fight them at all. We would rather die than to kill others.|Tee Watt Kaa|<ref name="DoP" />}}
[[File:Lurmen_village.png|thumb|right|285px|Lurmen on Maridun]]
Lurmen communicated using spoken [[language]] and were able to speak [[Galactic Basic Standard]]. Most Lurmen were pacifistic, such as the Lurmen colony that settled on the uncharted world of Maridun. [[Tee Watt Kaa]], the leader of this colony, believed in expressing non-violence and non-aggressive solutions. The colonists of Maridun led peaceful lives, harvesting giant seedpods to use for shelter and nourishment.<ref name="SWE" />

==History==
===Emigration to Maridun===
{{Quote|We colonized this system to find solace from your wretched war. We came here to find peace.|Tee Watt Kaa, to Ahsoka Tano|<ref name="JC" />}}
During the [[Clone Wars]], a group of peace-seeking Lurmen immigrants<ref name="VE" /> led by Tee Watt Kaa landed on Maridun, an uncharted world, in order to avoid the violence and bloodshed.<ref name="SWE" /> The Clone Wars had spread to the Lurmen [[homeworld]], [[Mygeeto]],<ref name="Mygeeto" /> by [[21 BBY]].<ref name="Build Falcon 45">{{BuildFalconCite|45|Guide to the Galaxy}}</ref>

Also in 21 BBY,<ref name="21BBYDate">{{TCWcanondates|113}}</ref> [[Jedi General]]s [[Aayla Secura]] and [[Anakin Skywalker]], as well as Skywalker's [[Padawan]], [[Ahsoka Tano]], crash-landed on the [[planet]] after their ship was [[Battle of Quell|damaged]] by [[Confederacy of Independent Systems|Separatist]] forces. Skywalker was badly injured from the wreck, and the group had come under attack by [[mastiff phalone]]s, so Tano and Secura set off in search of help. Upon coming across the Lurmen village, they requested aid, but Tee Watt Kaa refused to get his people involved, demanding that the [[Jedi]] leave.<ref name="JC" />

Tano, desperate for her master's survival, pleaded with Kaa to help Skywalker. Complying to Tano, Kaa sent his son, Wag Too, to heal Skywalker. Kaa, however, demanded that a Jedi stay behind to ensure that Wag Too would not be taken. Agreeing, Secura remained with the Lurmen. When Tano and Wag Too returned to the camp, they found it was under attack by two mastiff phalones. Wag Too, not wanting to harm the animals, tied the creatures up and stopped Tano from [[Death|killing]] them. Taking Skywalker to the Lurmen camp, Wag Too began his healing process.<ref name="JC" />

===Separatist battle===
{{Quote|I am General Lok Durd of the Separatist Alliance. ''[...]'' You are now under the protection of the Separatist Alliance. I congratulate you on your good fortune.|Lok Durd|audio=LokDurd1.flac|<ref name="DoP" />}}
[[File:Durd_in_command.png|thumb|left|285px|Lok Durd commanding his droid forces on Maridun.]]
Soon after the arrival of the Jedi, Separatist [[general]] [[Lok Durd]] came to the planet and announced the Separatists' occupation. The Lurmen hid the Jedi and their [[Galactic Republic|Republic]] allies, refusing to fight against the Separatists. The Jedi left the village, and Wag Too, against his father's wishes, assigned a Lurmen scout, [[Tub]], to track them and make sure they got off-world.<ref name="DoP" />

Durd, who saw the Lurmen's extreme pacifism, decided to use the Lurmen village as a testing grounds for his [[Defoliator Deployment Tank|new experimental weapon]]. The Republic forces, having witnessed Durd's initial demonstration, warned Kaa of the upcoming onslaught, but the elder refused to use violence, saying he would rather die. As a last resort, the Jedi used [[Deflector shield generator|shield generators]] to [[Defense of Maridun|protect]] the village, sparing it from the weapon. In response, the Separatist general ordered his army of [[battle droid]]s to attack, forcing the Jedi to defend the unmoved Lurmen.<ref name="DoP" />

The droids eventually overwhelmed the Jedi, compelling Wag Too to rally his<ref name="DoP" /> fellow younger<ref name="Lurmen SWE">{{SWE|groups|lurmen|Lurmen}}</ref> Lurmen who didn't truly believe in absolute pacifism. The Lurmen attacked and tied up the droids, leaving them to the Republic forces. The remaining droids left the village, and the Lurmen, led by Kaa, thanked the Jedi.<ref name="DoP" />

===End of the war===
[[File:Mygeetobridgebattle.png|thumb|right|285px|Mygeeto, during the Outer Rim Sieges]]
Home to a long lasting [[Mygeeto Campaign|campaign]],<ref name="Build Falcon 45" /> Mygeeto remained the site of heavy fighting all the way to the [[Outer Rim Sieges|final days]] of the war, during which time the [[Fourth Battle of Mygeeto]] was fought on its frigid terrain.<ref name="RotS">{{Film|III}}</ref> Sometime later, the Lurmen were enslaved.<ref name="CL">[[Star Wars: Complete Locations (2016)|''Star Wars: Complete Locations'']]</ref>

==Lurmen in the galaxy==
{{Quote|You can disallow installations, but then you need to pay some poor programmer to sit around doing stuff a half-blind Lurmen could manage.|Jessa Spanjaf, to [[Merei Spanjaf|her daughter]]|<ref name="RitR">''[[Servants of the Empire: Rebel in the Ranks]]''</ref>}}
Lurmen were native to Mygeeto.<ref name="DoP" /> During the [[High Republic Era]], Lurmen [[Jedi Master]] [[Giktoo Nelmo]] was highly respected for her considerable [[Force power]]s.<ref name="ITD" /> The tribe present on Maridun during the Clone Wars avoided contact with the galactic community. Generally, Lurmen were thought to be unintelligent; however, during the Separatist occupation of Maridun, Lok Durd admitted that the Lurmen were smarter than they appeared.<ref name="DoP" /> Seventeen years later, during the [[Imperial Era|Age of the Empire]], [[Jessa Spanjaf]], a data-security specialist on [[Lothal]], claimed that a programmer's tasks were menial enough for even a half-blind Lurmen to manage.<ref name="RitR" />

During the Imperial Era, a Lurmen was part of the [[Unidentified Queen of Ktath'atn|Queen of Ktath'atn's]] menagerie of [[alien]]s who had been implanted with [[Abersyn symbiote]]s.<ref name="Star Wars 31">[[Star Wars (2015) 31|''Star Wars'' (2015) 31]]</ref> During the [[New Republic era]], a Lurmen appeared in a [[Resistance]] [[propaganda]] poster called ''[[Defenders]]'', which called on recruits to protect those who couldn't defend themselves.<ref name="Propaganda">''[[Star Wars Propaganda: A History of Persuasive Art in the Galaxy]]''</ref>

One Lurmen male, [[Jat Kaa]], owned a [[Jat Kaa's Coolers|company]] that sold various beverages at [[Black Spire Outpost]] on the planet [[Batuu]] sometime during the [[First Order-Resistance War|war]] between the Resistance and the [[First Order]].<ref name="Galaxy's edge">''[[Star Wars: Galaxy's Edge: Traveler's Guide to Batuu]]''</ref>

==Behind the scenes==
The Lurmen species first appeared in "[[Jedi Crash]]," the thirteenth episode of ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]''. [[George Lucas]] nurtured the idea of lemur-like aliens since [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|Star Wars: Episode IV ''A New Hope''.]]<ref name=":0">''[[The Art of Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]''</ref> The species, developed by [[Erik Tiemens]] and designed by [[Robert E. Barnes]] and [[Sang Jun Lee]],<ref name="Difficult Design">{{SW|url=news/back-from-the-drawing-board-recycled-star-wars-aliens|text=Back from the Drawing Board: Recycled ''Star Wars'' Aliens|int=Back from the Drawing Board|nolive=1}}</ref> was originally set to appear in [[Star Wars: Episode III Revenge of the Sith|''Star Wars'': Episode III ''Revenge of the Sith'']] but was ultimately cut during production.<ref name="Art">{{InsiderCite|issue1=83|issue2=85|The Art of Revenge of the Sith|The Art of ''Revenge of the Sith''}}</ref> According to Lee, their design dated back to [[2002]] and was inspired by a trip Lucas took to the San Francisco Zoo, where he saw a flag advertising its lemur exhibit. Being one of his more favored species, the Lurmen initially survived multiple iterations of story changes, though Lucas struggled to find a way to include them in the movie. They were first set to appear on [[Utapau]], but this was switched to Mygeeto before being scrapped altogether in [[2003]].<ref name="LDB">{{DB|species|lurmen|Lurmen}}</ref>

For the Clone Wars show, the Lurmen were created by [[Jackson Sze]] and [[Kilian Plunkett]].<ref name=":0" />

